{{also|flyout}}
==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|fly<,,flew,flown> out|past2_qual=baseball|past2=flied out|past_ptc2_qual=baseball|past_ptc2=flied out}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To travel by airplane to a destination.
#: ''I'm going to '''fly out''' to see you.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To have someone travel rapidly to a destination on an airplane.
#: '''''flew''' him '''out''' to New York''
# To rapidly emerge
#:  {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Pieces '''flew out''' in all directions.''
# {{lb|en|intransitive|baseball}} To become [[out]] by hitting a [[fly ball]] which is caught/
#: ''Jones '''flied out''' to right field.''
#  {{lb|en|intransitive}} To fly into a [[rage]]; to become very angry.

====Usage notes====
* In the baseball sense, the simple past and past participle "flied out" are commonly used in order to avoid confusion with the other senses. For example, the sentence "Jones flew out to right field" could mean either that Jones hurried quickly to [[right field]] or that Jones became out by hitting a fly ball to right field, which was caught. On the other hand, "Jones flied out to right field" can only refer to a fly ball. this is a very important thing to know if you dont have a good understanding of this concept it will be hard for you. 

====Coordinate terms====
* {{sense|baseball}} {{l|en|pitch out}}, {{l|en|foul out}}, {{l|en|ground out}}, {{l|en|throw out}}, {{l|en|tag out}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|baseball}} An instance of flying out
#: ''A '''fly out''' was recorded on the scorecard.''

